    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "“Nobody is going to read a 33-part story on social media,” Brandon Stanton thought at first. But Stephanie Johnson’s story, a 76-year-old New Yorker, was very "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "compelling",
            "ref": "v-compelling"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". Some even thought her life should be made into a TV series."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Johnson’s story is just one of thousands that Stanton, a street photographer, has shared on his blog and social media accounts."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Stanton spent his first two years after college working in finance. In his free time, he took photos to relax. When he lost his job, Stanton thought of turning his hobby into a career. He packed his bags and moved to New York City. He planned to photograph 10,000 "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "random",
            "ref": "v-random"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " people on the street and place those photos on a map of the city. But over time, the project became less about the map and more about the people. Visitors to his blog were more interested in the conversations Stanton was having with his "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "subjects",
            "ref": "v-subject"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "“I realised that my talent wasn’t just in photography,” Stanton said. “I had stopped 5,000 strangers. I could make them feel "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "comfortable",
            "ref": "v-comfortable"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " enough to open up before taking their picture.”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Stanton went from spending two minutes with a subject to having hour-long conversations. Sometimes, his subjects told him things they had never told anyone else. They can choose to remain "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "anonymous",
            "zh": "匿名的"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", but many choose to share their faces and their stories—with the world."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Over the past ten years, Stanton has gained more than 20 million followers. He has also published four books. Some of them have been bestsellers. But the photographer isn’t slowing down. He wants to meet more people and discover more untold stories."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "i1",
        "type": "imageRef",
        "image": "img1"
      }
    ]
  },
